<resources>
    <!-- `F-112`. THE brand gold, as the icon's whole field. The mark now
         carries its own contrast instead of borrowing it from the wallpaper. -->
    <color name="swip_icon_background">#C9A227</color>

    <!--
      `F-158`. The floating bubble's own palette.

      Deliberately NOT the theme tokens. The bubble is drawn over an arbitrary
      app, so it has no ground to borrow contrast from and must bring its own —
      the same rule `swip_tokens.dart` states for the camera overlays. These
      two values are SWIP's ink and SWIP's gold, and they stay put whether the
      user is in Paper or Foil.
    -->
    <!--
      `F-189`. **Inverted.** The ground was ink with a gold hairline and a gold
      disc inside it, which left a 9 dp ink ring around the mark:

      > *"make the border of logo on the launcher icon less black bordered and
      > more yellow"*

      Gold field, ink mark is what `ic_launcher_foreground` already does and
      what the owner sees on their home screen every day, so the floating
      button now matches the app icon instead of being its photographic
      negative. The inner disc is gone with it — see `swip_bubble_bg.xml`.

      The hairline is ink at 25%: enough to hold an edge against a white app,
      far too little to read as a border. It is the one thing keeping a gold
      circle from dissolving into a bright screenshot, which is the same
      contrast argument `F-172` settled for the hover chrome.
    -->
    <color name="swip_bubble_ground">#C9A227</color>
    <color name="swip_bubble_edge">#40060507</color>

    <!--
      `F-193`. **The bubble's own border, separate from `swip_bubble_edge`.**

      > *"add a border to that icon, like half of the last circle that you have
      > put in"* — prompt 51
      > *"so this is basically a bit of black border to the launcher icon"*

      `swip_bubble_edge` above is ink at **25%**, which is a hairline that keeps
      a gold circle from dissolving into a white screenshot and is deliberately
      too faint to read as a border. That is the right value for the snooze
      target — a dim ring around a near-opaque ink disc — and the wrong value
      for the thing the owner is asking to see.

      So this is a second token rather than a change to the first. Repointing
      `swip_bubble_edge` would have put a solid ink ring on `swip_snooze_target`
      too, where the fill is already ink at 72%: ink on ink, and the target
      would have lost its outline in the same edit that gave the bubble one.
      **Two drawables sharing a colour is not the same as two drawables wanting
      the same colour.**

      Full ink, not a tint. `F-189` deleted a **9 dp** ink ring because the
      owner asked for *"less black bordered and more yellow"*; 2 dp of it is a
      border you can see without going back to a bubble that is mostly ring.
      The field stays gold, which was the point of that change.
    -->
    <color name="swip_bubble_border">#060507</color>
</resources>
